Rewrite README as matching Chinese and English versions

Split into README.md (Chinese) and README.en.md (English), cross-linked at the
top. Both cover the same ground in the same order.

WHY A REWRITE RATHER THAN AN EDIT
    Two things had gone wrong with the old README and both were worse than they
    sounded.

1. SEVERAL COMMANDS DID NOT RUN AT ALL
    The training walkthrough used flags that do not exist:

      train_deep_hash_v3_64.py  documented --student / --teacher
                                actual    --embedding-cache / --teacher-cache
      train_pca_64.py           documented --embedding / --output
                                actual    --embedding-cache / -o
      post_optimize_codes_recall_64.py  documented --deep-hash
                                actual    --model-type / --model-path

    Someone following the old README would hit an argparse error on step 4 of 9.
    Every command in both new files was checked against the real parsers, and a
    checker script confirmed all 34 flag references exist. Commands are also now
    written so they can be pasted as-is.

2. THE BENCHMARK TABLE CONTRADICTED THE PAPER
    The old README carried this table:

      PCA-52                        0.45 0.62 0.68 0.74
      Deep Hash v3 (h256)           0.51 0.66 0.72 0.78
      Deep Hash v3 + Post           0.53 0.68 0.74 0.80
      Ensemble (4 models)           0.55 0.71 0.77 0.83
      MIH coarse + bge-large rerank 0.78 0.86 0.90 0.93

    The paper's tab:main-results-cn says Recall@10 for PCA-52 is 0.5318, and for
    Deep Hash without post-processing it is 0.42-0.43 -- the paper explicitly
    notes that a bare Deep Hash is WORSE than PCA-52, and that post-processing is
    what rescues it. The README instead showed Deep Hash at 0.72, comfortably
    above PCA-52, which inverts the paper's central finding.

    It also mixed two different measurements. The R@1/R@5/R@10/R@20 rows come
    from a per-K curve where PCA-52 is (0.45, 0.62, 0.68, 0.74) -- so the PCA row
    is a curve, while the "Deep Hash v3" rows list a single number repeated
    across K in a way that does not match any figure in the paper.

    I could not establish where the old numbers came from. Rather than guess,
    both new READMEs use the paper's table verbatim, cite it as the source, and
    state the counter-intuitive result plainly: without stage-three
    post-processing, Deep Hash is worse than the linear baseline.

    One number is kept but framed correctly -- the 0.2750 measured on the new
    bilingual vocabulary is labelled as not comparable to the paper's table,
    because the vocabulary is larger (6,109 vs 3,109) and mixed-language, and no
    post-processing has been run on it.

